  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSee TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING section below.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will slowly retreat tonight and cloud cover looks\nminimal or thin enough to allow for another very cold night.\nWidespread teens and 20s look likely again.\n\nAll the attention however will be on the expansive storm\ntracking up the East Coast, that will bring impacts to the the\nlocal area Thu night thru Fri. I will cover the impacts outside\nof coastal flooding here.\n\nA strong east northeast LLJ will lift northeast along the \ncoast, but will be weakening as it reaches the forecast area. \nHow quickly that weakening happens will largely determine what \ntype of impacts we see beyond the Seacoast and adjacent York \nCounty. It does look gusty along the coast all the way to \nPenobscot Bay, but generally topping out around 35 mph. Saco Bay\nand especially the Seacoast however could see some locally \nhigher gusts. There is a window late Thu evening were some 45+ \nmph gusts are possible for an hour or two. There is also an \nincreasing model signal for downslope winds to be quite gusty \nacross the southern half of NH. This signal is strongest from \nthe Sunapee region down to the Monadnocks. The inversion height \nis just about at the right level to help enhance the remnants of\nthe LLJ and increase those gusts up to 40 to 50 mph in a \nlocalized north/south stripe. I had to include some HRRR/RRFS \ninto the forecast to capture this smaller scale feature. These \nwinds will taper quickly early Fri as that LLJ continues \nDowneast.\n\nAlso given how cool and dry this air mass is, we should see a \nrobust cold air dam develop as precip moves in Thu evening.\nAcross the higher terrain and up into the upper Kennebec River\nValley some of this precip could start as snow. That will be\nbrief however as warm advection will overwhelm the mid levels.\nForecast soundings are primarily freezing rain as that warmer\nair invades aloft, but QPF will be the main limiting factor. A\ndry slot will be racing north, so we may only see 0.25 to 0.5\ninches QPF before the steady precip shuts off. That will mean it\nwill be awfully hard to hard to get ice amounts very high once\nyou account for marginal/warming temps and shedding. But given\nthat even a small amount of ice on surfaces can be hazardous a\nWinter Weather Advisory has been issued for the areas most\nlikely to see some light accumulations thru Fri morning.\n\nElsewhere a steady period of cold rain is expected. Near the\nsouthwestern Maine coast down to the Seacoast some enhanced\nrainfall totals are possible near the coastal front. Here\namounts over an inch are possible. Once the dry slot moves thru\nsome lingering shower or drizzle likely lingers thru the day\nFri, followed by upper level low showers Fri evening into Sat.\n\nBeyond that another storm is looking more likely than not early\nnext week. This is shaping up to be another inland track with\nrain the most likely precip type. Given the range the ensemble\nsignal is pretty strong for some measurable rainfall.",
